      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\u2032 & =\\frac{x + 1}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =x + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int x + 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\frac{y^{2}}{2} & =- 2 x + \\frac{\\left(x + 3\\right)^{2}}{2} - 6+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2}=- 2 x + \\frac{\\left(x + 3\\right)^{2}}{2} - 6+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ Solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^2 & =- 4 x + \\left(x + 3\\right)^{2} - 12+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{- 4 x + \\left(x + 3\\right)^{2} - 12+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{- 4 x + \\left(x + 3\\right)^{2} - 12+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C$ is an arbitrary real constant.",
