      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t}=-2s^2.$ For $s\\ne 0$.\n\n$s=($ {{field-1}} $+K)^{-1}$, where $K$ is an arbitrary real constant.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s^2}\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}s}{\\mathrm{d}t} & =-2 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{s^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s & =\\int -2\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{s} & =- 2 t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a general solution in implicit form. Solving for $s$ gives\n\n$$\ns=\\frac{1}{2 t+K}=(2 t+K)^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=-C$. Work on intervals where the denominator is nonzero.",
